        <div id="weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Why Weight Matters More Than Horsepower
          </h2>

          <p>
            Horsepower gets the headlines, but for most small-property work, weight is what limits a tractor. Pulling a box blade through compacted gravel, pushing into a pile of dirt with a loader or dragging a stump out of the ground depends on traction, and traction depends on weight on the tires.
          </p>

          <p>
            A lighter tractor will spin its tires before it runs out of engine power on many jobs. That is why two tractors with similar horsepower can feel very different in the field if one weighs several hundred pounds more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weight also affects stability. A heavier tractor is more stable when lifting loads with a loader. That is why ballast, such as a weighted box on the three-point hitch or liquid-filled rear tires, is recommended for loader work.
          </p>

          <p>
            When comparing tractors, look at the operating weight in the specifications, and compare it with the loader lift capacity and the size of implements you plan to run. A tractor that is too light for your heaviest job will be frustrating no matter how much horsepower it has.
          </p>

          <p>
            Loaded rear tires are a common way to add weight permanently. Tire shops fill them with a ballast liquid, adding several hundred pounds low on the tractor where it helps traction and stability most. Owners who do regular loader work often combine loaded tires with a ballast box for heavier lifts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hitch-pto" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Three-Point Hitch and PTO
          </h2>

          <p>
            The three-point hitch at the rear of the tractor attaches implements such as box blades, rear blades, tillers, mowers, post-hole diggers and ballast boxes. Hitches come in categories that define pin sizes and dimensions. Most compact tractors use Category 1; many sub-compacts use a limited version that accepts smaller implements.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the lift capacity at the hitch points, which determines how heavy an implement the tractor can raise. Heavier implements such as large rotary tillers or box blades need more lift capacity and a heavier tractor.
          </p>

          <p>
            The power take-off (PTO) transmits engine power to implements such as rotary cutters, tillers and snow blowers. The rear PTO usually runs at 540 rpm. A mid PTO runs mid-mount mowers and some front snow blowers on sub-compacts.
          </p>

          <p>
            PTO horsepower, the power available at the PTO shaft, is lower than engine horsepower. Implement manufacturers list required PTO horsepower, so check that your tractor meets the requirement for implements you plan to run.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pay attention to top-link and lift-arm adjustment when you hook up an implement. A box blade or rear blade that is not level side to side or front to back will grade poorly no matter how good the tractor is. Most owners learn to set the top link length and sway chains by trial on a short stretch of driveway.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="transmissions" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Hydrostatic or Gear Transmission
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most sub-compact and many compact tractors use hydrostatic transmissions, controlled by foot pedals for forward and reverse. They are easy to learn, ideal for loader work where you constantly change direction, and smooth for mowing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gear transmissions, available on some compact tractors, are more efficient at transmitting power to the ground and implements. They are suited to long, steady work such as tilling or brush-hogging large fields, but require clutching and shifting.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most small-property owners, hydrostatic is the better choice because of loader work and ease of use. Owners with large fields and a lot of PTO work may prefer gear drive.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some tractors offer a range selector for hydrostatic transmissions, with low, mid and high ranges for different tasks. Use low range for loader and ground work and high range for travel and mowing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cruise control and pedal settings vary between brands. Some hydrostatic tractors offer cruise for mowing and long travel. Others let you link engine speed to pedal position. Try the controls at the dealer, ideally with a loader mounted, to see which arrangement feels natural to you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="loaders" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Front Loaders
          </h2>

          <p>
            A front loader is the attachment that makes a compact tractor most useful. It moves gravel, mulch, soil, firewood and snow, lifts heavy items and, with forks, handles pallets and bales.
          </p>

          <p>
            Loader lift capacity is listed in the specifications, usually at the pivot pin and sometimes at a point forward of it. Capacity at the bucket edge is lower. Compare both figures and consider what you plan to lift.
          </p>

          <p>
            Quick-attach systems let you swap buckets, forks and grapples quickly. A skid-steer style quick attach is widely used and opens up a large range of attachments.
          </p>

          <p>
            Always use rear ballast when operating the loader, as manufacturers specify. Without it, the rear wheels can lift, and the tractor can tip forward or become unstable. Carry loads low when traveling and avoid slopes with a raised loader.
          </p>

          <p>
            Joystick control is standard on most modern loaders and lets you lift and curl the bucket at the same time. A third-function valve adds hydraulics for a grapple or other powered attachment, which is worth adding at purchase if you plan to move brush, logs or rocks, since retrofitting later costs more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="backhoes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Is a Backhoe Worth It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A backhoe turns a tractor into a small excavator, useful for digging trenches, removing stumps, installing drainage, digging footings and shaping ponds. Sub-compact TLB packages such as the Kubota BX23S come with a backhoe designed for quick attachment.
          </p>

          <p>
            Backhoes are expensive, often adding several thousand dollars to the cost. If you only need to dig occasionally, renting a mini excavator or hiring a contractor may be cheaper.
          </p>

          <p>
            Backhoes also add weight to the rear of the tractor, which serves as ballast for loader work. Many TLB owners leave the backhoe on for loader work for that reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have regular digging tasks such as drainage, stumps or trenching, a backhoe can pay for itself over time. If not, spend the money on implements you will use more often.
          </p>

          <p>
            Digging depth and reach are listed in the backhoe specifications. Check them against your intended jobs. A sub-compact backhoe digs trenches and holes for drainage and small footings well but is not a substitute for a full-size excavator on major earthwork.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="implements" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Implements and Their Real Cost
          </h2>

          <p>
            The tractor is only part of the cost. Most owners spend significant additional money on implements. Common first purchases include a front loader, a box blade for grading driveways, a rotary cutter for brush and pasture, a rear blade for snow and grading, a tiller for gardens and pallet forks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Match implement size to the tractor. An implement that is too large or heavy strains the tractor, reduces stability and may exceed hitch lift capacity. Implement manufacturers list recommended tractor horsepower and hitch categories.
          </p>

          <p>
            Used implements can save money. Box blades, rear blades and rotary cutters are simple and often available secondhand. Check for cracks, worn bearings and damaged PTO shafts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Budget for implements when choosing a tractor. A less expensive tractor with the right implements is often more useful than a more expensive tractor with none.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan your implement purchases around seasons. A rear blade or front snow blower is worth having before the first storm; a rotary cutter is most useful in early summer; a tiller pays off in spring. Buying out of season can also bring better prices on new and used implements.
          </p>

