        <div id="snow" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Using a Compact Tractor for Snow
          </h2>

          <p>
            For owners with long driveways, snow clearing is often the job that tips the decision toward a tractor. A sub-compact can run a front-mounted snow blower from the mid PTO, a rear-mounted snow blower from the rear PTO, a front blade or simply the loader bucket. Each approach suits different snow conditions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Front-mounted blowers let you drive forward and see the work, but they require a mid PTO and a compatible mounting frame. Rear-mounted blowers are common and less expensive but require driving in reverse, which many owners find tiring on long driveways. Some tractors can be fitted with a cab or a sunshade-style enclosure to make cold-weather operation more comfortable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Blades and loader buckets work well for wet, heavy snow and short driveways, but they pile snow at the edges, which becomes a problem after several storms. Blowers throw snow farther and keep driveways clear through a long winter.
          </p>

          <p>
            For winter work, use tire chains or turf-to-industrial tires for traction, keep rear ballast on, and use winter diesel or an anti-gel additive. Store the tractor where the battery stays warm enough to start reliably.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tires" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Tires: Turf, Industrial or Agricultural
          </h2>

          <p>
            Compact tractors typically offer three tire types. Turf tires have a shallow tread that protects lawns and are ideal if the tractor mows often, but they slip in mud and snow. Agricultural tires, often called R1, have deep lugs for maximum traction in fields and mud but tear up lawns.
          </p>

          <p>
            Industrial tires, often called R4, are the middle ground. They offer good traction in dirt, gravel and snow while being less damaging to lawns than agricultural tires. Many owners who use their tractor for loader work and some mowing choose industrial tires.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tire choice affects stability and lift as well. Wider tires spread weight and reduce ruts on soft ground. Filled tires add ballast. Consider where the tractor will spend most of its time when choosing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Changing tires later is possible but expensive, so it is worth choosing carefully at purchase. Ask the dealer whether you can swap tire types as part of the deal.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="dealer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. Choosing a Dealer
          </h2>

          <p>
            For compact tractors, the dealer matters as much as the brand. A tractor will need service, parts and possibly warranty repairs, and a nearby dealer with a good service department makes ownership far easier.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask about service turnaround times, whether they offer pickup and delivery, parts availability and technician experience. Talk to other owners in your area about their experiences.
          </p>

          <p>
            Deere and Kubota have broad dealer networks and strong resale value. Kioti, Massey Ferguson and LS offer competitive pricing and warranties but have thinner networks in some areas. A great local dealer for a less common brand can be better than a distant dealer for a major brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dealers often offer packages including the tractor, loader and implements, sometimes with financing promotions. Compare the total package price and ask about delivery, setup and training.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask about setup and delivery charges, which can add a meaningful amount. Some dealers include a walkthrough of controls and safety features, first service and a period of free pickup. These extras are worth factoring into the comparison between dealers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            13. Tractor Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Rollovers are the leading cause of tractor-related deaths. Keep the rollover protective structure (ROPS) raised and wear the seat belt whenever operating the tractor, except when the ROPS must be lowered for clearance, and then operate with extreme caution and no seat belt.
          </p>

          <p>
            Avoid steep slopes, ditches and embankments. Keep loads low when traveling, use rear ballast with the loader, and drive slowly on uneven ground.
          </p>

          <p>
            PTO shafts can cause severe injuries if clothing or limbs become entangled. Keep shields in place, wear close-fitting clothing and never step over a rotating PTO shaft. Disengage the PTO, shut off the engine and remove the key before working on implements.
          </p>

          <p>
            Never allow passengers on a tractor unless it has an approved seat. Keep children away from operating tractors. Read the operator's manual and consider a tractor safety course if you are new to tractors.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mount and dismount using the steps and grab handles, facing the tractor, and never jump off. Lower the loader and any implements to the ground, set the parking brake and remove the key when you leave the seat, even briefly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="upkeep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            14. Upkeep
          </h2>

          <p>
            Follow the manufacturer's maintenance schedule. Tractors have hour meters, and service intervals are based on hours. Typical tasks include engine oil and filter changes, fuel filter replacement, air filter cleaning or replacement, hydraulic and transmission fluid and filter changes, and greasing pivot points.
          </p>

          <p>
            Grease fittings on the loader, front axle and three-point hitch at the hour interval your manual gives, which is short for loader pins that see daily use. Check tire pressure and look for leaks in hydraulic hoses.
          </p>

          <p>
            Diesel engines need clean fuel. Use fresh diesel, consider an additive in cold weather to prevent gelling and drain water from the fuel separator as the manual directs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Store the tractor under cover. Sun and rain degrade paint, seats and hoses. A battery maintainer helps keep the battery charged during long periods of inactivity.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep a simple log of hours, fluids and filters. It helps you stay on schedule, supports warranty claims and adds to resale value. Many owners record service on the inside of the tool box lid or in a phone note.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="budget" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            15. Budget
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sub-compact tractors with a loader typically cost roughly $18,000 to $30,000 at the time of writing, depending on brand and options. Adding a mid-mount mower deck and backhoe increases the cost significantly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compact tractors with a loader typically cost roughly $25,000 to $45,000 or more, depending on horsepower and options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Implements add thousands more. A basic set of box blade, rotary cutter and rear blade can add several thousand dollars. Pallet forks, grapples and specialty implements add more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Used tractors can save money, but have them inspected by a mechanic and check hours, maintenance records and the condition of the loader, hydraulics and transmission.
          </p>

