        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            16. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing by horsepower instead of weight</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Horsepower gets attention, but traction and stability come from weight. A light tractor spins its tires before it uses all its power on many jobs. Compare operating weight, loader capacity and hitch lift capacity against your heaviest expected work. Check the spec sheet for operating weight.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Picking the brand before the dealer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A tractor needs service and parts for many years. A great local dealer with fast turnaround can matter more than the badge on the hood. Visit dealers, ask about service and talk to local owners before choosing. Service delays during busy seasons are costly. Ask how long typical repairs take.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the loader without ballast</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Operating a loader without rear ballast can lift the rear wheels and make the tractor unstable. Use a ballast box, a backhoe or loaded rear tires as the manufacturer specifies, and carry loads low. Follow the manual's ballast guidance for every lift. Never travel with the bucket raised high.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting implement costs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The tractor is only part of the expense. Box blades, rotary cutters, rear blades and forks add thousands. Budget for the implements you will actually use and size them to the tractor. Plan the implement budget alongside the tractor budget. Used implements can stretch the budget further.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the ROPS folded down</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The rollover protective structure only protects you if it is up and you wear the seat belt. Fold it down only when clearance requires it, and then operate with extreme caution. Most tractor fatalities involve rollovers, and a folded ROPS gives no protection. Raise it again as soon as clearance allows.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            17. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which compact tractor fits most small properties?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most two-to-five-acre properties, a sub-compact such as the Kubota BX23S, John Deere 1025R or Kioti CS2520 handles mowing, loader work and snow. The BX23S is the strongest pick if you need a backhoe. For five to twenty acres with pasture and heavier ground work, a compact like the Kubota L2502 is a better fit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">At what acreage does a tractor make sense?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the jobs more than the acreage. Owners of two acres or more who regularly move gravel, mulch or soil, grade driveways, brush-hog or clear snow often find a sub-compact worthwhile. If you mostly mow, a lawn tractor or zero-turn is cheaper and faster. Make a list of yearly jobs before deciding.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I choose between sub-compact and compact?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Choose a sub-compact for smaller properties with mowing, light loader work and snow clearing, especially if storage space is limited. Choose a compact for larger properties with heavier ground work, larger implements and more serious loader use. If you are on the edge, many owners recommend the larger tractor. Storage space and gate widths often settle the question.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does a compact tractor cost with implements?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sub-compacts with a loader typically cost about $18,000 to $30,000, and compacts with a loader roughly $25,000 to $45,000 or more at the time of writing. Implements such as a box blade, rotary cutter and rear blade can add several thousand dollars more. Dealer package deals can reduce the total.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a backhoe attachment?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you have regular digging jobs such as trenching, stump removal or drainage, a backhoe can pay for itself. If you only need to dig occasionally, renting a mini excavator or hiring a contractor may be cheaper. Backhoes also serve as rear ballast for loader work. Weigh how often you would really dig.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which tractor brands hold resale value?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                John Deere and Kubota tractors generally hold their value well thanks to strong brand recognition and dealer networks. Kioti, Massey Ferguson and LS often cost less up front but typically have lower resale values. Condition, hours and maintenance records also strongly affect resale. A well-kept tractor of any brand sells more easily.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a small tractor need four-wheel drive?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most small-property owners, yes. Four-wheel drive improves traction for loader work, slopes, snow and soft ground. Most sub-compact and compact tractors with loaders come with 4WD as standard. Two-wheel drive is mainly found on basic models used for flat, dry mowing and light work. It is also the safer choice on slopes.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
